              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for the master's degree, then the Portuguese for the doctorate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: rever",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rever — \"to revise, to go over; to see again\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Revi a matéria toda no fim de semana. I revised all the material at the weekend."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Conjugated like ver: revejo, revês, revê. In Portugal it is the usual word for revising for exams."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rever is built from re- \"again\" and ver \"to see\", inherited from Latin vidēre \"to see\"; compare Latin revidēre \"to see again, to go to see again\" (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
